[8] *Once upon a time*, to be sure, but *once upon a time* doesn't mean *any time*. It means *a long time ago*. So once upon a time is just an old-fashioned way of saying *a long time ago*. 

> 
> 
> Once upon a time, the world was flat.
> 
> 
> 

That's a perfectly true sentence. It just means that people used to think the world was flat. They were wrong about that, but they weren't wrong *at the time*. 

> 
> 
> Once upon a time I knew someone who said the world was round.
> 
> 
> 

This could mean two things. It could mean that he knew someone *in the past* who thought the world was round. Or it could mean that he knew someone in the present who thinks the world is round. In either case it would be true.<|im_end|><|endoftext|><|endoftext|><|endoftext|><|endoftext|><|endoftext|><|endoftext|><|endoftext|><|endoftext|><|endoftext|><|endoftext|><|endoftext|><|endoftext|><|endoftext|>
[9] *Once* to *"a time long ago,"* that's one way to start it, isn't it? It could go on like this: "Once upon a time, in a far-off land, a clever young man named [name] was the son of a wealthy merchant and the heir to his vast fortune." 

**But, once you've got them hooked with that opening line, you've got to keep up the pace.** In other words, don't let the story drag on and on. If your story doesn't have to do anything with *you*, don't get too personal. You want to be as impersonal as possible so people can relate to your story.

